# XBD 9.5 Regular Expression Grammar
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The tokens and productions are exercised by the tests of 9.3 and 9.4 and
# XSH.regcomp; this module adds the lexical corner cases and checks that
# grammatical expressions are accepted.

assertion  XBD.9.5-grammar.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.1
class      required
since      1992
says       A back-reference token is a backslash and one digit: \(a\)\10 is the
           back-reference \1 followed by the character 0; digits outside an
           interval are ordinary characters: 1\{2\} and 1{2} repeat the 1, 12\{2\} and
           12{2} repeat the 2, 0\{0\}1 and 0{0}1 match 1.
test       t/001-lexical-digits.c

assertion  XBD.9.5-grammar.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.1
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.2
class      required
since      1992
says       Characters that are special elsewhere (circumflex, left bracket, equals
           sign, colon, period, backslash, dollar sign, asterisk,
           left parenthesis) are valid collating symbols: [[.^.]], [[.[.]], [[...]],
           [[.=.]], [[.:.]], [[.\.]], [[.$.]], [[.*.]] and [[.(.]] match the one character each and no other.
test       t/002-collating-meta.c

assertion  XBD.9.5-grammar.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.3
class      required
since      1992
says       Expressions made of the productions of the grammar and nothing it calls
           undefined are accepted by regcomp(): BREs (anchors, groups, back-
           references, intervals, quoted characters, bracket forms) and EREs
           (alternation, nested groups, every duplication symbol, anchors, quoted
           characters, ordinary closing bracket and brace). The repetition modifier
           is tested with XSH.regcomp.033.
test       t/003-valid-forms.c
